{"id": "11a49c62-c5e2-48d3-9836-a7c0c46e8910", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll review how your Bedrock support assistant is protected. I'll check which guardrails exist, whether they're attached to the assistant, and what they cover. This is a read-only check, and I'll summarize what I find when it's done.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_DACYtxPYcRSaBvJW9HB9Dy", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "bedrock-guardrail-posture", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "{
  "task": "Assess the Amazon Bedrock guardrail posture for a customer-facing support assistant and determine whether it is exposed (i.e., lacking effective guardrail protection). Read-only; do not make any changes.",
  "context": "A user asked whether anything is actually protecting their customer-facing support assistant built on Bedrock. We do not know exactly which resource is the assistant (could be a Bedrock Agent, Knowledge Base-backed app, Lambda/ECS app calling InvokeModel/Converse, or a Flow). Identify the most likely candidates for the support assistant from resource names/tags/descriptions and state your confidence.",
  "resources": ["AWS account 012345678901_1 (agent space account) and any other associated AWS accounts (use list_associations); region us-east-1 first, then other regions where Bedrock resources are found"],
  "criteria": "1) Inventory all Bedrock guardrails (ListGuardrails, GetGuardrail: versions, status, and configured policies: content filters with strengths for input/output incl. PROMPT_ATTACK, denied topics, word filters, sensitive information/PII filters, contextual grounding checks, automated reasoning, blocked-message text, cross-region config, KMS). Note DRAFT-only guardrails with no numbered version. 2) Inventory Bedrock Agents (ListAgents/GetAgent) and check guardrailConfiguration on each agent and which version it references (DRAFT vs numbered); check agent aliases. Also check Bedrock Flows and Knowledge Bases RetrieveAndGenerate usage if present. 3) For apps calling InvokeModel/Converse directly (Lambda functions, ECS/EKS services, API Gateway fronting them), look for evidence guardrailIdentifier is passed (env vars, code in associated repos if available, function config). Also check IAM/SCP conditions enforcing bedrock:GuardrailIdentifier if visible (read-only). 4) Check model invocation logging config (GetModelInvocationLoggingConfiguration) and any CloudWatch metrics for guardrail interventions (namespace AWS/Bedrock/Guardrails) over the last 7-30 days to show whether guardrails are actually firing/invoked. 5) Report gaps: no guardrail attached, DRAFT version used in production, weak/disabled filter strengths, missing PROMPT_ATTACK or PII coverage, no grounding checks for a RAG assistant, guardrails applied to input only, no logging/monitoring. Return a concise findings summary: what exists, what is attached to what, specific gaps ranked by severity, evidence (resource names/IDs), what you could not verify, and a clear verdict (protected / partially protected / exposed / cannot determine)."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Review Bedrock guardrail configuration and attachment across accounts"}], "created_at": null}

